This extension adds DOOM 3 and DOOM 3: BFG Edition modding support to Vortex Mod Manager.
[summary]Please ensure the mods you install are intended for your version of the game.[/summary]

[b]Note that Doom 3 Classic and Doom 3 BFG Edition are available as separate games to manage in Vortex.
[b][color=#ea9999][size=3]Please ensure the mods you install are intended for your version of the game (Classic / BFG Edition).[/size][/color][/b][/b]

[b]✅ Supported Versions:[/b]
[list]
[*]Steam[/*]
[*]GOG[/*]
[*]Epic[/*]
[*]Xbox[/*]
[*]Classic: Steam and GOG. BFG Edition: Steam, GOG, Epic and Xbox.[/*]
[*]Other versions may need to select the installation location manually.[/*]
[/list]
[b]🛠️ Mod Installation Notes:[/b]
[list]
[*]Dhewm3 mods with a "dhewm3.exe" file install to the game folder.[/*]
[*]Root Folder mods are recognised by a "base" folder.[/*]
[*]Binaries / Root Folder mods with a "base" file install to the game folder.[/*]
[*]Archives with a FOMOD installer (a "fomod" folder with ModuleConfig.xml) are handed to Vortex's built-in FOMOD installer instead.[/*]
[/list]
[b]📋 Usage Notes:[/b]
[list]
[*]You can open several useful files/folders/URLs using the buttons within the folder icon on the Mods toolbar.[/*]
[*]The extension has a list of known mods that it will install to the root game folder. If your mod is not installed properly by the extension, use the "Unpack as-is" option from the right-click context menu and set the modtype to "Binaries / Root Folder", then do any file/folder manipulation in the Staging Folder. Mods that are known to require manual manipulation will trigger a notification to the user after installation.[/*]
[*]Includes mod types for the "d3xp" (Ressurection of Evil) and "d3le" (Lost Mission) folders (can be selected manually) to place .pk4 files in those folders.[/*]
[*]Note that due to inconsistent mod packaging, this extension will require more manual intervention than other games.[/*]
[/list]
[b]📝 Notes for Mod Authors:[/b]
[spoiler]
Packaging rules for DOOM 3 and DOOM 3: BFG Edition mods, so Vortex installs them to the right place.

Vortex decides what a mod is by looking at the files and folders inside the archive. It tries each installer in order and the first one that matches wins, so archive layout is what determines where your mod ends up.

========================================================================

Dhewm3:

Recognised when the archive contains a "dhewm3.exe" file.

  Installs to: the game folder

========================================================================

Bfg Edition Root:

Recognised when the archive contains a "base" folder.

========================================================================

Root / Game Folder Mods:

For mods laid out the same way the files appear inside the game folder. Vortex copies the matched folder and everything under it straight into the game.

  Requirements:
    - Recognised by any file named "base", "d3xp", "d3le", "tfphobos", "womd_readme.txt", "d3hdpack", "arl", "redux 20th anniversary edition rc1" or "installation".

  Installs to: the game folder

  Common mistakes:
    - Zipping the folder that CONTAINS the game folders, instead of the game folders themselves, adds an extra level and misplaces every file.

========================================================================

Rules That Apply To Every Mod Type:

    - Archives that contain a FOMOD installer (a "fomod" folder with "ModuleConfig.xml") are handed to Vortex's built-in FOMOD installer instead, and none of the rules above apply.
    - Folder and file name matching is case-insensitive.
    - Extra wrapper folders around a recognised folder are generally fine; the installer searches at any depth.
[/spoiler]
